# CRLF Injection Lab - Practice Report

## Summary
Local Flask/http.server lab demonstrating CRLF injection leading to:
- Header injection
- Session fixation via Set-Cookie
- Response splitting leading to XSS

## Environment
- Python 3.13
- http.server (raw, no protection)
- Endpoint: GET /redirect?url=<payload>

## Vulnerability
The `url` parameter is placed directly into the `Location` header without sanitization. The server does not filter `\r` or `\n` characters.

## Payloads and Results

### 1. Header Injection
Payload: `https://example.com%0d%0aX-CRLF-Test:%20Injected`
Result: New header `X-CRLF-Test: Injected` injected into response.

### 2. Session Fixation
Payload: `https://example.com%0d%0aSet-Cookie:%20session=malicious`
Result: Malicious cookie `session=malicious` set in response.

### 3. Response Splitting → XSS
Payload: `https://example.com%0d%0a%0d%0a<html><body><script>alert(1)</script></body></html>`
Result: Response body split; attacker-controlled HTML/JS injected.

## Impact
- Session fixation / cookie manipulation
- Cache poisoning
- XSS via response splitting
- Potential account takeover

## Remediation
- Reject input containing `\r` or `\n`
- Use allowlist for redirect URLs
- Encode output in headers
- Use modern frameworks (Flask/Werkzeug already protect against this)

## Lessons Learned
- Modern frameworks (Werkzeug) block CRLF by default
- Raw servers (http.server) are vulnerable
- Response splitting is the most dangerous variant
